From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan <ryan@esterlines.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:46:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LibURL+LibWeb+LibWebView+WebContent: Run WebAuthn ceremonies
 in the UI process

WebContent now sends WebAuthn requests to the UI process, which owns the
authenticator part of every ceremony. Before starting one, it checks the
request against its own records of the documents a page hosts: the
document must be the active document of its navigable and hosted by the
asking page, have a non-opaque origin in a secure context, and be allowed
the feature by its permissions policy, and the RP ID must be its host or
a registrable domain suffix of it. It works out sameOriginWithAncestors
and the top-level origin itself, and builds the client data from them,
so a renderer can only ever ask for credentials for its own site.

WebAuthnCoordinator runs one ceremony at a time, in a view that is
visible and focused. It hands the request to every authenticator an
AuthenticatorSource reports, follows the client's rules for user
verification, discoverable credentials and PINs, retries with the appid
extension's AppID, applies the attestation conveyance preference, and
asks the user through ViewImplementation's new WebAuthn prompt hooks.
Errors that would tell a page whether a credential exists go to the user
rather than the page. Ceremonies end on their timeout, when the page
aborts them, or when their document goes away or their view is hidden.

The UI process also needs a little CBOR, to build attestation objects and
read credential public keys for getPublicKey(), and the HTML check for a
registrable domain suffix, which moves from LibWeb to URL::Host.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
